<h1>Dekopon on Kubernetes</h1>
<p>One pod runs the broker as UID 65532 and the gateway as UID 65533, joined by one Unix socket. Peer authentication and descriptor passing need one kernel, so don't split the daemons across pods or scale them against one state claim.</p>
<p>Coming from OpenClaw or Hermes? <a href="/deploy/mac/">Run on a Mac</a>.</p>
</header>
<nav class="guide-jumps" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#install">Install and probe</a><a href="#gateway">Enable chat</a><a href="#providers">Add providers</a><a href="#upgrade">Upgrade</a>
</nav>
<section id="install"><span class="compat-anchor" id="status" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="startup" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Chart {{ release.chart.publishedVersion }} · application {{ release.chart.publishedAppVersion }}</h2>
<p>You need native sidecars, a ReadWriteOnce StorageClass and a namespace that admits a root init container with only <code>CHOWN</code> and <code>FOWNER</code>: Pod Security <code>baseline</code>, not <code>restricted</code>. That container copies the broker's files, which the broker refuses as Kubernetes mounts them. <a href="{{ deploy }}#kubernetes">What the values don't make obvious ↗</a></p>
<p>This installs the broker alone, with its read-only CLI probe and no credentials. Read <code>rendered.yaml</code> before you install.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Install the default broker and check its socket"><code># Set these two values for your cluster.
export KUBE_CONTEXT=your-context
export STORAGE_CLASS=your-rwo-class
helm template dekopon oci://ghcr.io/dekopon-agents/charts/dekopon \
  --version {{ release.chart.publishedVersion }} --namespace dekopon \
  --set-string state.storageClassName="$STORAGE_CLASS" \
  --set-string image.tag={{ release.tag }} --set-string image.digest={{ release.imageDigest }} &gt; rendered.yaml

helm upgrade --install dekopon \
  oci://ghcr.io/dekopon-agents/charts/dekopon \
  --version {{ release.chart.publishedVersion }} --kube-context "$KUBE_CONTEXT" \
  --namespace dekopon --create-namespace \
  --set-string state.storageClassName="$STORAGE_CLASS" \
  --set-string image.tag={{ release.tag }} --set-string image.digest={{ release.imageDigest }} --wait --timeout 10m
kubectl --context="$KUBE_CONTEXT" -n dekopon \
  rollout status deployment/dekopon --timeout=5m
kubectl --context="$KUBE_CONTEXT" -n dekopon exec deployment/dekopon \
  -c broker -- dekopon-brokerd probe --socket /run/dekopon/broker.sock</code>
</pre>
<p><strong>Expected:</strong> the probe exits 0 with no output. The default broker grants nothing, so it has nothing to run.</p>
</section>
<section id="gateway"><span class="compat-anchor" id="pod" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="security" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="one-pod" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="files" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Chat starts when the broker maps UID 65533</h2>
<p><a href="{{ site.repository }}/tree/main/examples/conditional-write">examples/conditional-write ↗</a> has a broker configuration, Cedar policy, gateway configuration and catalog for one Slack sender. Adapt it, then:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Map the gateway at the broker.</strong> Put the broker's <code>*.yaml</code> and <code>*.cedar</code> files in a ConfigMap and name it in <code>broker.configDirectory.configMap</code>. With <code>broker.configDirectory.peers</code>, on by default, the chart maps UID 65533 to the principal <code>dekopon-gatewayd</code> with an attestor grant. Without that mapping the broker refuses the gateway.</li>
<li><strong>Enable the gateway.</strong> Set <code>gateway.enabled: true</code> with <code>gateway.config</code> and <code>gateway.catalog</code>. The chart names any key you still owe.</li>
<li><strong>Give each daemon only its own credentials.</strong> Chat and model tokens go in Secrets that <code>gateway.env</code> references; provider credentials go in <code>broker.credentials</code>. A token typed into Helm values lands in the release, where <code>helm get values</code> prints it.</li>
<li><strong>Prove a real turn.</strong> Send a chat message that invokes a harmless granted capability, then <a href="/how-it-works/#trace">find its decision and execution records</a>. A passing probe doesn't prove the model, chat service or provider credentials work.</li>
</ol>
<p>That attestor grant covers every subject namespace: compromise the gateway and it speaks for any sender, with that sender's grants.</p>
<div id="credentials">
<h3>The ChatGPT credential is seeded once</h3>
<p>Log in on a workstation with <code>dekopon-gatewayd auth chatgpt login</code>, export the Secret and set <code>gateway.chatgpt</code>. The gateway rotates the refresh token, so after the first refresh your exported Secret is a retired token: never copy it back over the live one. A broker ChatGPT credential needs a second, separate login. <a href="{{ deploy }}#kubernetes">Export and re-seed steps ↗</a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="providers"><span class="compat-anchor" id="state" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>The image ships two probe providers</h2>
<p>The image carries <code>cli-probe</code> and <code>http-probe</code>; review and install anything else yourself. For a managed provider set, create the state claim first and name it in <code>state.existingClaim</code>, then enable <code>broker.providerSet</code> and <code>broker.providerSync</code>. A hook Job syncs the set before each install and upgrade, and an init container compiles it before the broker starts. A failed sync leaves the old pod serving. Under Argo CD, run the Job as a <code>Sync</code> hook after its ConfigMap. <a href="{{ deploy }}#kubernetes">Hook ordering ↗</a></p>
<p>A digest proves byte identity, not who published the component.</p>
</section>
<section id="upgrade">
<h2>Both daemons upgrade from one release</h2>
<p>Their protocol has no cross-release negotiation. <code>helm upgrade</code> replaces both in the one pod; <code>configCheck.enabled</code> runs both daemons' <code>check</code> on the new configuration before the release applies. Rolling back undoes no external effect, and <code>helm rollback</code> skips the pre-upgrade hooks. <a href="{{ deploy }}#upgrades">Upgrade and rollback steps ↗</a></p>
</section>
